## Security Review of **app.js**

Below is a line‑by‑line analysis of the current `app.js` file, identification of the most relevant OWASP Top 10 risks, and concrete recommendations (with ready‑to‑copy code snippets) to harden the application.

---

### 1️⃣ Current State (what the file does)

```js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.get('/health', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ status: 'ok', timestamp: new Date().toISOString() });
});

app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.json([
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
  ]);
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server running on port 3000');
});

module.exports = app;
```

**What we see**

| Feature | Security Impact |
|---------|-----------------|
| **No input handling** – the only endpoints are `GET /health` and `GET /users`. | No injection surface today, but future routes could introduce it. |
| **Public `/users` endpoint** – returns all user records without any authentication or authorization. | **A01: Broken Access Control** (data leakage). |
| **No security‑hardening middleware** – Helmet, CORS, rate‑limiting, etc. are missing. | **A04–A06: Insecure Design / Security Misconfiguration / Missing Security Headers**. |
| **No logging or error handling** – uncaught errors will bubble to the console. | **A09: Insufficient Logging & Monitoring**. |
| **Plain‑HTTP default** – `app.listen(3000)` does not enforce HTTPS. | **A02: Cryptographic Failures** (transport‑level). |
| **No explicit dependency version control** – may be pulling vulnerable versions of Express or other transitive dependencies. | **A06: Vulnerable & Outdated Components**. |

Even though the code is tiny, the absence of *any* security controls is a red flag for a production‑grade service.

---

## 2️⃣ OWASP Top‑10 Mapping

| OWASP ID | Issue in current code | Why it matters |
|----------|----------------------|----------------|
| **A01 – Broken Access Control** | `/users` is public and leaks personal data. | Attackers can enumerate users, harvest emails, launch phishing attacks. |
| **A02 – Cryptographic Failures** | No TLS enforcement; data in transit is plain‑text. | Man‑in‑the‑middle can sniff or tamper with traffic. |
| **A03 – Injection** | No injection now, but future route parameters are not validated/sanitized. | SQL/NoSQL/command injection could compromise the backend. |
| **A04 – Insecure Design** | No authentication/authorization framework, no rate limiting. | System is trivially exploitable when expanded. |
| **A05 – Security Misconfiguration** | Missing security‑related HTTP headers, no CORS policy, default Express error pages. | Increases attack surface, enables XSS/CSRF. |
| **A06 – Vulnerable & Outdated Components** | `express` version is not pinned; no automated audit shown. | Known exploits could be automatically leveraged. |
| **A07 – Identification & Authentication** | No login or token verification at all. | Any client can call any endpoint. |
| **A08 – Software & Data Integrity** | No integrity checks for code or dependencies (e.g., `npm audit`). | Supply‑chain attacks possible. |
| **A09 – Logging & Monitoring** | No request/response logging, no error handling. | Incidents go unnoticed. |
| **A10 – Server‑Side Request Forgery** | Not present now, but any future outbound request must be whitelisted. | Prevents abuse of internal services. |

---

## 3️⃣ Recommended Fixes (with code)

Below is a **progressive** set of changes you can apply **directly** to `app.js`. Feel free to split them into separate modules (e.g., `middleware/auth.js`) as the project grows.

### 3.1 Install needed dependencies

```bash
npm install helmet cors express-rate-limit morgan jsonwebtoken dotenv
# OPTIONAL for production HTTPS termination (e.g., when behind a reverse proxy)
# npm install spdy # if you want to serve HTTPS directly from Node
```

> **Why**  
> * `helmet` – sets safe HTTP headers (CSP, X‑Frame‑Options, HSTS, etc.).  
> * `cors` – defines which origins may call your API.  
> * `express-rate-limit` – mitigates brute‑force, DoS, credential‑stuffing attacks.  
> * `morgan` – structured request logging.  
> * `jsonwebtoken` – lightweight JWT‑based authentication.  
> * `dotenv` – keeps secrets out of source code.

---

### 3.2 Harden the server (middleware stack)

```js
require('dotenv').config();               // <-- load .env variables early
const express = require('express');
const helmet = require('helmet');
const cors   = require('cors');
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');
const morgan = require('morgan');
const jwt    = require('jsonwebtoken');

const app = express();

// -------------------
// 1️⃣ Common security middlewares
// -------------------
app.use(helmet());                     // sets a solid set of security headers
app.use(cors({                         // restrict origins – adjust for your front‑end
  origin: process.env.ALLOWED_ORIGINS?.split(',') || '*',
  methods: ['GET','POST','PUT','DELETE','OPTIONS'],
}));
app.use(express.json());               // body parser for JSON payloads

// -------------------
// 2️⃣ Rate limiting (global & per‑route)
// -------------------
const apiLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, // 15 minutes
  max: 100,                // limit each IP to 100 requests per window
  standardHeaders: true,  // Return rate limit info in the `RateLimit-*` headers
  legacyHeaders: false,
});
app.use('/api/', apiLimiter); // apply to all future API routes

// -------------------
// 3️⃣ Request logging (structured, works with many log aggregators)
// -------------------
app.use(morgan('combined')); // or use 'tiny' for less verbosity

// -------------------
// 4️⃣ Simple JWT auth middleware (replace with your own auth strategy)
// -------------------
const authGuard = (req, res, next) => {
  const authHeader = req.headers.authorization;
  if (!authHeader?.startsWith('Bearer ')) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Missing or malformed Authorization header' });
  }
  const token = authHeader.split(' ')[1];
  try {
    // `JWT_SECRET` must be a strong, randomly‑generated string stored in .env
    const payload = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
    req.user = payload;   // attach user info to request for downstream handlers
    next();
  } catch (err) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid or expired token' });
  }
};
```

---

### 3.3 Protect Sensitive Endpoints

```js
// Health endpoint – keep public but limit rate
app.get('/health', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ status: 'ok', timestamp: new Date().toISOString() });
});

// Example: login endpoint (issues a JWT)
// In a real app replace the hard‑coded user check with DB lookup + password hashing (bcrypt)
app.post('/login', (req, res) => {
  const { email, password } = req.body;
  // ----- SIMPLE MOCK CHECK -------------------------------------------------
  const mockUser = { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com',
                     // password: 'password123' would be hashed in production
                     role: 'admin' };
  if (email !== mockUser.email || password !== 'password123') {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid credentials' });
  }
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  const token = jwt.sign(
    { sub: mockUser.id, name: mockUser.name, role: mockUser.role },
    process.env.JWT_SECRET,
    { expiresIn: '1h' }
  );
  res.json({ token });
});

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Protected users endpoint – requires a valid JWT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
app.get('/users', authGuard, (req, res) => {
  // In a real system fetch from DB, apply RBAC, hide sensitive fields, etc.
  const users = [
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
  ];
  res.json(users);
});
```

> **What we fixed:**  
> * `/users` now requires a verified JWT (`authGuard`).  
> * Added a very simple `/login` to illustrate token issuance.  
> * The JWT secret is loaded from `.env` and never checked into source.

---

### 3.4 Centralised Error Handling (prevents stack‑trace leaks)

```js
// 5️⃣ Generic error handler – must be after all routes
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  // Log the full error for developers/monitoring
  console.error(err);
  // Do **not** expose internal details to the client
  const status = err.status || 500;
  const message = status === 500 ? 'Internal Server Error' : err.message;
  res.status(status).json({ error: message });
});
```

---

### 3.5 Enforce HTTPS (if you terminate TLS at the Node process)

If you choose to serve TLS directly, add:

```js
const fs = require('fs');
const https = require('https');

if (process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production') {
  const credentials = {
    key:  fs.readFileSync(process.env.SSL_KEY_PATH,  'utf8'),
    cert: fs.readFileSync(process.env.SSL_CERT_PATH, 'utf8')
  };
  https.createServer(credentials, app).listen(443, () => {
    console.log('HTTPS server listening on port 443');
  });
} else {
  // dev fallback (plain HTTP)
  app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on http://localhost:3000'));
}
```

*If you are behind a reverse‑proxy (NGINX, Cloud‑flare, ALB, …) you can skip the Node‑TLS part and just enforce `app.set('trust proxy', 1)` plus `helmet.hsts()`.*

