## Additional Hardening Checklist (Secrets‑Specific)

| ✅ Item | Recommended Action |
|--------|--------------------|
| **Never commit raw secrets** | Add `.env*` and any `*-secret*` patterns to `.gitignore`. |
| **Rotate regularly** | Use the rotation capabilities of your secret manager (e.g., AWS Secrets Manager automatic rotation). |
| **Audit access** | Enable CloudTrail (AWS) / audit logs in Vault / GCP audit logging to track who/what read the secret. |
| **Version control of secret values** | Most managers keep a version history; avoid manual edits. |
| **Least‑privilege IAM** | The compute role (EC2, ECS task, Lambda, Cloud Run) should have permission **only** to read the specific secret ARN. |
| **Separate dev / prod secrets** | Use distinct secret identifiers (`myAppJwtSecret-dev` vs `myAppJwtSecret-prod`). |
| **Zero‑trust network** | Ensure the service can only reach the Secrets Manager endpoint via a VPC endpoint or private network. |
| **Inject via environment variables only at runtime** | Do **not** write the secret to a temporary file; keep it in memory. |
| **Test secret‑unavailability** | Simulate a secret‑fetch failure and verify the app exits cleanly (prevents running with a default key). |
| **Monitor for “canary” values** | If you keep synthetic canary keys (like those in `.env.test`), set up alerting on any access or accidental exposure. |

