## 2️⃣ Why loading secrets from a file is risky

| Issue | Impact |
|-------|--------|
| **File stored in source control** (`.env.test` is present) | Anyone with repo read access instantly obtains all credentials. |
| **Plain‑text on disk** (including backups, CI artefacts) | An attacker who gains host access can read them with trivial permissions. |
| **No rotation / audit** | Changing a secret means editing a file and re‑committing – no systematic rotation, no audit trail. |
| **Environment‑specific values are coupled to code** | Deployments to different clouds, staging vs prod, or container images must each carry the file – hard to manage. |
| **Lack of policy enforcement** (e.g., IAM least‑privilege) | Keys stored in the same file may have broader permissions than a given service actually needs. |

These problems map directly to **OWASP A02 – Cryptographic Failures** (exposure of secrets) and **A06 – Vulnerable & Outdated Components** (relying on a library (`dotenv`) that does not provide secret‑storage guarantees).

