## 4. Checklist for Ongoing Security Maintenance

| ✅ | Item |
|----|------|
| **Authentication** | All endpoints that expose data or modify state require proper auth (JWT, OAuth, etc.). |
| **Authorization** | Verify role‑based access (e.g., only admins see email addresses). |
| **Input Validation** | Use a schema validator (Joi/Zod) on every route that accepts user data. |
| **Secure Headers** | Helmet is enabled; custom CSP added if you serve HTML. |
| **Transport Security** | Enforce HTTPS at the reverse‑proxy level; optionally redirect HTTP to HTTPS inside Express. |
| **Rate Limiting** | Global limiter + per‑endpoint limits (especially login). |
| **Error Handling** | No stack traces sent to the client; detailed logs kept for ops. |
| **Logging** | Structured request logging (pino) with request IDs. |
| **Dependency Management** | `npm audit`, Dependabot, or Snyk integrated into CI. |
| **Configuration** | Secrets stored in `.env`/environment, validated by `dotenv-safe`. |
| **Testing** | Add integration tests that assert protected routes reject unauth‑ed requests and that rate limiting works. |
| **Docker/Deployment** | Run the container as a non‑root user, set `NODE_ENV=production`. |
| **Regular Review** | Schedule a quarterly security review of code and third‑party packages. |

Following these recommendations will dramatically reduce the attack surface of your application and align it with modern OWASP Top 10 guidance. If you add new routes or features, repeat the same checklist to keep the codebase secure. 🚀

